{{also|Sortie}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2022|July|10}}

===Etymology===
{{PIE word|en|upó}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*ser-|id=bind|*h₃reǵ-}}
[[File:Hawker Hurricane Mk I taking off in the night.jpg|thumb|A [[w:Hawker Hurricane|Hawker Hurricane Mark I]] [[fighter plane]] of [[w:No. 85 Squadron RAF|No. 85 Squadron]], {{w|Royal Air Force}}, at [[Debden]] in [[Essex]], [[England]], U.K., about to [[take off]] on a [[night]] sortie ''(sense 1.2)'' to [[intercept#Verb|intercept]] [[enemy]] [[aircraft]] on 14 March 1941.]]

The {{glossary|noun}} is borrowed from {{bor|en|fr|sortie|t=act of exiting; exit, way out; (''military'') sally, sortie}}, the {{glossary|past}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|fr|sortir|t=to exit, go out}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=184969|date=December 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|sortir}}, from {{der|en|la|sortīrī}}, the {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|active}} {{glossary|infinitive}} of {{m|la|sortior|t=to cast or draw lots; to choose, select; to distribute, divide; to obtain, receive; to share}}, from {{m|la|sors|t=something used to determine chances, a lot; casting or drawing of lots; decision by lot; a share}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ser-|t=to bind, tie together; a thread}}), possibly influenced by {{m|la|surrēctus|t=arisen, having been caused to arise; gotten up, having been gotten up}}, the {{glossary|perfect}} {{glossary|passive}} participle of {{m|la|surgō|t=to arise, get up, rise}}, from {{m|la|subrigō|t=to lift up; to straighten}}, from {{m|la|sub-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘beneath, under’}} + {{m|la|regō|t=to direct, guide, steer; to govern, rule; to manage, oversee}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-|t=to right oneself, straighten; just; right}}).

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=184970|date=December 2020|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

